WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:07.560
Okay, so what is except sometimes we call it minus in other databases.

00:07.560 --> 00:09.680
But in SQL server we call it except.

00:09.680 --> 00:16.640
So it can go and return a distinct rows from the first query that are not found in the second query.

00:16.640 --> 00:22.160
So from this definition, we can understand that the order of the queries can affect the final result.

00:22.160 --> 00:27.760
There is a first query and a second query, so it is the only set operator where you have to pay attention

00:27.760 --> 00:29.600
to the order of the queries.

00:29.600 --> 00:33.960
And as well it's like the others it can go and remove the duplicates from the result sets.

00:34.000 --> 00:36.080
Alright, again we have this very simple example.

00:36.080 --> 00:41.640
We have two sets, five customers, five employees and there is the same persons as a customer and as

00:41.640 --> 00:42.960
employees, Kevin and Mary.

00:42.960 --> 00:45.840
So now we're going to go and combine those two sets using the excepts.

00:45.840 --> 00:47.240
Or sometimes we call it minus.

00:47.480 --> 00:52.800
So it says it's going to return unique rows in the first table that are not in the second table.

00:52.800 --> 00:53.680
So what's going to happen.

00:53.680 --> 00:54.560
What is the first table.

00:54.560 --> 00:56.600
Let's say the customers on the left side.

00:56.600 --> 01:00.760
So here we have five persons Joseph, Mark, Anna, Kevin and Mary.

01:00.760 --> 01:05.130
So now the rule is we need the customers that are not employees.

01:05.130 --> 01:10.770
So it's safe for Joseph, Mark and Anna because they are not existing in the second set.

01:10.770 --> 01:13.410
That's why SQL can return those three values.

01:13.410 --> 01:17.770
But now for the two customers given and marry here there is an issue given and marry.

01:17.770 --> 01:21.290
They are members of the second sets, the second table, the employees.

01:21.530 --> 01:27.810
That's why SQL can go and exclude them from the output because they are not fulfilling the rule.

01:27.810 --> 01:34.130
So the output will get only three customers and all the values from employees and the common values

01:34.130 --> 01:38.650
between customers and employees will be excluded from the output.

01:38.650 --> 01:40.690
So this is how the accepts works.

01:40.730 --> 01:41.050
All right.

01:41.050 --> 01:42.730
So let's have a very simple SQL task.

01:42.730 --> 01:47.850
And it says find the employees who are not customers at the same time okay.

01:47.890 --> 01:49.290
So let's see how we're going to solve that.

01:49.330 --> 01:51.290
We're going to stay with the same queries as usual.

01:51.290 --> 01:53.530
We have the employees and the customers.

01:53.730 --> 01:57.810
But instead of having union all we're going to use the set operator except.

01:57.810 --> 02:02.450
So now since we are using except we have to make sure that the order of the queries are correct.

02:02.450 --> 02:08.740
So the first query is the employees, which is correct because we have to find the employees who are

02:08.740 --> 02:10.100
not customers at the same time.

02:10.100 --> 02:11.940
So we are focusing on the employees.

02:11.940 --> 02:15.300
The first table is correct and the second table is customers.

02:15.300 --> 02:20.740
If the task says find the customers who are not employees at the same time, then we have to go and

02:20.740 --> 02:21.300
switch it.

02:21.340 --> 02:23.460
We have first to query the customers.

02:23.460 --> 02:26.500
So now everything is correct, let's go and execute it.

02:26.500 --> 02:30.540
And now in the output we see three employees who are not customers at the same time.

02:30.540 --> 02:32.780
So we have Carl, Frank and Michael.

02:32.820 --> 02:35.340
But as we know we have five employees Kevin and Mary.

02:35.340 --> 02:38.660
They are not here in the result because they are customers as well.

02:38.700 --> 02:42.620
So now let me show you what can happen if I just switch those informations.

02:42.620 --> 02:45.620
So we start with customers and then with employees.

02:45.660 --> 02:46.980
Let's go and execute it.

02:46.980 --> 02:49.020
As you can see we're going to get completely different results.

02:49.060 --> 02:51.780
Now we are getting customers informations.

02:51.820 --> 02:56.500
And now in the output we got three customers who are not employees at the same time.

02:56.500 --> 02:58.460
This is not what we want from this task.

02:58.460 --> 03:00.540
So if you do it like this it's going to be incorrect.

03:00.540 --> 03:03.460
So pay close attention here to the order of the query.

03:03.460 --> 03:05.140
So now let's go and correct it.

03:05.140 --> 03:07.940
So we can have first employees and then customers.

03:07.980 --> 03:09.140
Let's execute it.

03:09.140 --> 03:13.140
And now let's go and understand how SQL execute the except operator.

03:13.180 --> 03:16.740
Alright, so again we have the results from the two queries or from two tables.

03:16.740 --> 03:18.900
And now we are doing except between them.

03:18.900 --> 03:20.660
So let's see how SQL gonna execute it.

03:20.700 --> 03:26.420
It's going to take as usual first the names from the first query from the employees and put it in the

03:26.460 --> 03:26.980
output.

03:27.020 --> 03:31.220
And now it's going to present data only from the first query in the output.

03:31.260 --> 03:34.620
And it's going to go and use the customers only as a check.

03:34.780 --> 03:38.580
So SQL will not put any data or rows from the customers.

03:38.580 --> 03:42.900
It will just use the second query as a lookup in order to check the data.

03:42.900 --> 03:45.940
So it's going to start with that first employee, Frank Li.

03:46.100 --> 03:48.180
Do we have frankly in the customers?

03:48.180 --> 03:49.460
Well, no, we don't have it.

03:49.460 --> 03:53.340
That's why SQL can accept it and put it in the output.

03:53.340 --> 03:56.540
And then in the next step it's going to go to the second employee and check.

03:56.580 --> 03:59.020
As you can see we have it already in the customers.

03:59.020 --> 04:00.820
So SQL going to go and ignore it.

04:00.820 --> 04:03.060
It's not allowed to be in the output.

04:03.060 --> 04:04.380
The same thing for Mary.

04:04.420 --> 04:06.500
We have it as well in the customers.

04:06.500 --> 04:09.100
That's why it will not be presented in the output.

04:09.340 --> 04:12.500
So Michael we don't have a Michael in the customers.

04:12.780 --> 04:15.110
That's why it can be presented in the output.

04:15.390 --> 04:16.710
And as well for Carol.

04:16.750 --> 04:17.550
The same thing.

04:17.550 --> 04:21.910
We don't have Carol as a customer and we're gonna have it in the output.

04:22.030 --> 04:26.430
So as you can see, we will get data only from the first table, and the second table only going to

04:26.430 --> 04:29.110
be used in order to check the information from it.

04:29.110 --> 04:31.110
So we don't have in the output any customers.

04:31.110 --> 04:32.110
It's only employees.

04:32.150 --> 04:34.790
So now let's check quickly what's going to happen if we switch the tables.

04:34.790 --> 04:39.630
So now we have the customers as the first table is going to take the columns from the first table.

04:39.630 --> 04:43.510
And it's going to start presenting the customers information in the output.

04:43.550 --> 04:46.430
And it's going to go and use the employees only as a lookup.

04:46.430 --> 04:47.950
So do we have Joseph.

04:47.990 --> 04:50.390
We don't have it in the employee.

04:50.790 --> 04:53.270
And then Kevin and Mary, we have it already in the employees.

04:53.270 --> 04:57.030
And Mark and Anna are not part of the employees.

04:57.030 --> 05:00.870
That's why SQL can go and present the results in the output like this.

05:00.870 --> 05:03.590
So now as you can see, SQL is focusing on the table customers.

05:03.590 --> 05:06.790
And we are getting data from the customers, not from the employees.

05:06.830 --> 05:08.630
Employees is only as a check.

05:08.630 --> 05:12.790
So with that we understand the order of the queries is very important for the exceptions.

05:12.790 --> 05:15.430
We will get different results if we have different order.

05:15.470 --> 05:15.790
All right.

05:15.790 --> 05:17.910
So that's all for the except operator.
